WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.966
Different security values have different requirements, so a secure RNG policy should not treat every random-looking value as the same thing.

2
00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:13.759
Tokens and session identifiers need enough unpredictability that they cannot be guessed in any practical way.

3
00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:19.914
They also need careful handling after generation: limited lifetime, appropriate storage, controlled logging, and clear invalidation behavior.

4
00:00:19.914 --> 00:00:27.517
A strong random token can still be mishandled if it is copied into logs or retained longer than the design expects.

5
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:32.586
Keys require approved generation workflows, storage protections, access control, rotation plans, and destruction rules.

6
00:00:32.586 --> 00:00:37.655
Nonces and initialization vectors have requirements that depend on the cryptographic construction being used.

7
00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:41.276
Some require uniqueness, some require unpredictability, and some require both.

8
00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:48.155
Salts are usually not secrets, but they still need to be generated correctly for the scheme that uses them.

9
00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:55.034
Challenges, correlation identifiers, and temporary names may have still different requirements based on whether guessing them has security impact.

10
00:00:55.034 --> 00:01:00.103
The safe lesson is to understand the use case before implementing the call site.

11
00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:06.621
Follow the requirements of the chosen cryptographic library, protocol, or organizational standard rather than inventing a local recipe.

12
00:01:06.621 --> 00:01:16.034
Document what the value is for, how much randomness is required, whether uniqueness matters, how it is encoded, how long it lives, and whether it can

13
00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:17.121
appear in telemetry.

14
00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:24.000
That documentation gives reviewers a concrete basis for judging whether the chosen random source and handling rules are appropriate.
